{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AhAPHvohqojjAC3EMRpDtg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Generic slotmap-style arena.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! A storage container that hands out [`Token`]s as opaque handles\n4\t//! to its values.  Tokens carry a generation counter so that\n5\t//! reusing a slot after removal invalidates any outstanding tokens\n6\t//! — the safe equivalent of the C `*mut T` use-after-free.\n7\t//!\n8\t//! Every Phase-4 collection that needs \"address an object by\n9\t//! handle, not by raw pointer\" sits on top of this — most notably\n10\t//! the `Quic` connection arena, which holds every live connection\n11\t//! and lets the secondary indexes (`connection_by_id`, `connection_by_net`, …)\n12\t//! store `ConnectionToken`s as their values.\n13\t//!\n14\t//! Bodies are `todo!()` — Phase 4 either hand-rolls the slotmap\n15\t//! (see the layout sketch in [`crate::hash`]) or wraps the\n16\t//! `slotmap` crate.  Either way the public API stays as below.\n17\t\n18\tuse core::marker::PhantomData;\n19\t\n20\tuse crate::Error;\n21\t\n22\t/// Opaque handle into an [`Arena<T>`].  `Copy`, 64 bits total.\n23\t///\n24\t/// The `T` parameter is purely a phantom — it stops you from using\n25\t/// a `Token<Connection>` to index into an `Arena<Stream>`.  The\n26\t/// runtime check still lives in the generation counter inside the\n27\t/// arena.\n28\t#[derive(Debug, Eq, PartialEq, Hash)]\n29\tpub struct Token<T> {\n30\t    idx: u32,\n31\t    generation: u32,\n32\t    _phantom: PhantomData<fn() -> T>,\n33\t}\n34\t\n35\t// `Copy` and `Clone` written by hand so they don't depend on `T:\n36\t// Copy`.  Tokens are always `Copy` regardless of what `T` is.\n37\timpl<T> Copy for Token<T> {}\n38\timpl<T> Clone for Token<T> {\n39\t    fn clone(&self) -> Self {\n40\t        *self\n41\t    }\n42\t}\n43\t\n44\t/// Slotmap-style arena that owns `T`s and addresses them by\n45\t/// generation-tagged tokens.\n46\t///\n47\t/// Phase 4 picks an implementation; the API below stays the same.\n48\tpub struct Arena<T> {\n49\t    /// Phase 4 fills the body.  Sketch:\n50\t    ///\n51\t    /// ```ignore\n52\t    /// struct Slot<T> {\n53\t    ///     generation: u32,\n54\t    ///     state:      SlotState<T>,\n55\t    /// }\n56\t    /// enum SlotState<T> { Free { next_free: Option<u32> }, Filled(T) }\n57\t    /// pub struct Arena<T> {\n58\t    ///     slots: Vec<Slot<T>>,\n59\t    ///     free:  Option<u32>,\n60\t    ///     len:   usize,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7c7e82c-a907-4d1f-a2c6-fbe9ac637b5e","uuid":"7b81eb9f-c0ca-427d-bfbd-d9e2ba08c90f","timestamp":"2026-05-04T18:27:54.729Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/arena.rs","content":"//! Generic slotmap-style arena.\n//!\n//! A storage container that hands out [`Token`]s as opaque handles\n//! to its values.  Tokens carry a generation counter so that\n//! reusing a slot after removal invalidates any outstanding tokens\n//! — the safe equivalent of the C `*mut T` use-after-free.\n//!\n//! Every Phase-4 collection that needs \"address an object by\n//! handle, not by raw pointer\" sits on top of this — most notably\n//! the `Quic` connection arena, which holds every live connection\n//! and lets the secondary indexes (`connection_by_id`, `connection_by_net`, …)\n//! store `ConnectionToken`s as their values.\n//!\n//! Bodies are `todo!()` — Phase 4 either hand-rolls the slotmap\n//! (see the layout sketch in [`crate::hash`]) or wraps the\n//! `slotmap` crate.  Either way the public API stays as below.\n\nuse core::marker::PhantomData;\n\nuse crate::Error;\n\n/// Opaque handle into an [`Arena<T>`].  `Copy`, 64 bits total.\n///\n/// The `T` parameter is purely a phantom — it stops you from using\n/// a `Token<Connection>` to index into an `Arena<Stream>`.  The\n/// runtime check still lives in the generation counter inside the\n/// arena.\n#[derive(Debug, Eq, PartialEq, Hash)]\npub struct Token<T> {\n    idx: u32,\n    generation: u32,\n    _phantom: PhantomData<fn() -> T>,\n}\n\n// `Copy` and `Clone` written by hand so they don't depend on `T:\n// Copy`.  Tokens are always `Copy` regardless of what `T` is.\nimpl<T> Copy for Token<T> {}\nimpl<T> Clone for Token<T> {\n    fn clone(&self) -> Self {\n        *self\n    }\n}\n\n/// Slotmap-style arena that owns `T`s and addresses them by\n/// generation-tagged tokens.\n///\n/// Phase 4 picks an implementation; the API below stays the same.\npub struct Arena<T> {\n    /// Phase 4 fills the body.  Sketch:\n    ///\n    /// ```ignore\n    /// struct Slot<T> {\n    ///     generation: u32,\n    ///     state:      SlotState<T>,\n    /// }\n    /// enum SlotState<T> { Free { next_free: Option<u32> }, Filled(T) }\n    /// pub struct Arena<T> {\n    ///     slots: Vec<Slot<T>>,\n    ///     free:  Option<u32>,\n    ///     len:   usize,","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":129}}}
